        ("How to prevent frozen pipes next time", """<ul><li><strong>Insulate exposed pipes</strong> in crawlspaces, attics, garages and exterior walls with foam sleeves; add heat tape on the worst runs.</li><li><strong>Let cold-wall faucets drip</strong> when temperatures drop below about 20°F, and open cabinet doors under sinks.</li><li><strong>Keep the heat at 55°F or above</strong>, even when away; set a smart thermostat alert for low temperatures.</li><li><strong>Disconnect hoses</strong> and shut off and drain exterior spigots and irrigation lines in the fall.</li><li><strong>Seal gaps</strong> where cold air reaches pipes: around vents, hose bibs, and foundation penetrations.</li><li><strong>If leaving for more than a few days in winter</strong>, shut off the main and drain the lines, or have someone check the house. This also protects your insurance claim.</li><li><strong>Install a leak sensor</strong> or a smart shut-off valve near the water heater and under sinks; some insurers discount for them.</li></ul>"""),
